1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
EVE Online slows time in star systems whose servers are overloaded. Pilots in the battle live fewer minutes than everyone else. What if the universe does this too — and we call it gravity?

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
More stuff in a region, more to compute, so that region updates slower. Let's test every link.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:28,000
One rule per region: match your neighbours' clock rate, minus your own workload.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The lag settles into a 1/r shape — Newton's shape. Honest caveat: any rule like this gives Poisson's equation. A construction, not a discovery.

5
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Remove gravity from the code. The only rule: tick your internal clock at the local rate.

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
It falls 16.00 units — exactly ½gt². This part is Einstein: things fall toward slower time.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Light bends toward heavy regions too — but lag alone gets exactly half the measured bending. Einstein's 1911 mistake. Space must stretch as well.

8
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
The hardest problem: gravity ignores complexity. If cost is clock ticks — and mass is a clock — cost is energy. Maybe.

9
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,000
No conclusions. Just observations. Code and sources are in the repo.
